    "rich_text_aMtaWr": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_iwj9Tm": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why Are DTF Transfers So Popular?",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text_JV6P88": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>DTF has become attractive to both individual creators and growing apparel businesses because it combines flexibility with relatively simple application.</p><h3>Vibrant, Full-Color Designs</h3><p>DTF is well suited to colorful artwork, gradients, illustrations, logos, and detailed graphics.</p><p>The white underbase also helps designs remain vibrant when they're applied to darker garments.</p><h3>Works on Many Fabrics</h3><p>One of DTF's biggest advantages is versatility.</p><p>Depending on the garment and transfer specifications, DTF can be used with materials such as:</p><ul><li>Cotton</li><li>Polyester</li><li>Cotton/poly blends</li><li>Denim</li><li>Canvas</li><li>Many other apparel fabrics</li></ul><p>Always test specialty or heat-sensitive materials before production.</p><h3>No Weeding</h3><p>Unlike heat transfer vinyl, a finished DTF transfer does not require you to weed individual pieces of the design.</p><p>That can make intricate artwork and larger production runs much easier to manage.</p><h3>Great for Detailed Artwork</h3><p>Small text, complex illustrations, multicolor logos, and photographic-style artwork can all be good candidates for DTF.</p><h3>Flexible Order Quantities</h3><p>DTF works particularly well when you need multiple designs, several sizes, or relatively short production runs.</p><p>For businesses producing many graphics at once, a <strong>DTF gang sheet</strong> can help arrange multiple designs on one larger sheet.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
